course: dictionary
lesson: 1141
title: Bribery and unfair deals
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bribe is money, a gift or a favour given to someone so they will do something dishonest or unfair for you. The word for this act is bribery. Look at the clues in the sentence: «Paying a guard or helper to let a prisoner escape is bribery and is a crime.» The guard is paid to break the rules, so bribery must mean paying for unfair help.
explain: The sentence gives another clue: «is a crime.» A crime is something the law says is wrong. Next: «In the book the washerwoman takes gold coins and asks to be tied up so it looks as if she was attacked.» She takes the coins and then tells a false story, which shows the bribe was a secret, dishonest deal.
explain: Bribery can also be a joke. Parents sometimes offer a treat to get children to do something, and people call this bribery in a playful way: «a bit of bribery with chocolate got the kids to tidy up». Nobody breaks the law there. Check the context to see whether the word is serious or funny.
fact: Bribery means giving or offering money, gifts or favours to get someone to act dishonestly or unfairly.
fact: In law, news and ethics, bribery is a crime, and officials, referees and judges must not accept bribes.
fact: Bribery is an uncountable noun, so we do not add an s to make it plural.
fact: In everyday family life, bribery is often used jokingly for a treat that helps a child do a task.
fact: Context tells you which meaning is meant: a crime in a court story, or a light joke at home.
exercise: «Paying a guard or helper to let a prisoner escape is bribery.» What is bribery here? | A kind gift for a birthday | Helping a prisoner find a new home | *Giving money or favours to get someone to act unfairly | A fair prize for good work
exercise: In the book, why does the washerwoman ask to be tied up? | To get a free meal | Her friends want to play a game | *So it looks as if she was attacked | To keep warm in the cold
exercise: «A bit of bribery with chocolate got the kids to tidy up.» What kind of bribery is this? | A bank robbery | A referee taking money from a team | A serious crime in a court | *A joke about a treat that helps children do a job
test: Which person must not accept bribes? | *A judge in a court | A bus driver on a sunny day | A gardener who grows roses | A baker who sells bread
test: Is bribery a crime in law? | No, it is only a joke | Only if the money is small | No, it is always allowed | *Yes, it is a crime
exam: Which sentence uses bribery in the light, joking way? | *A bit of bribery with chocolate got the kids to tidy up | The referee refused every bribe | The washerwoman took gold coins from the thief | Paying a guard to let a prisoner escape
